## State of the art 

There is no denying that frontier LLMs are disrupting and stirring up a lot of controversy around white collar jobs. One of the domain that seems to receive a lot of attention
is software engineering which, for a long time has been a very appealing domain: high salaries, an abundance of opportunities, status etc. Nowadays, however these jobs are under 
attack. 

A few years ago in one of his talks Rober C. Martin, uncle Bob - someone I really look up to, if you don't know him yet, look him up - stated something similar to Moore's Law: 
the number of software engineers is doubling every five years, which means that at every given moment half of the software engineers have less than 5 years of experience. I absolutely
loved this, however just like Moore's actual Law, this has followed suit - were no longer exponentailly adding transistors to a chip and the number of software engineers is no longer
doubling every five years. 

I read a lof of articles online and - maybe I'm biased because I am a software engineer - I keep getting a sense than people are actually happy that software engineers roles are nose diving
and that people are loosing their jobs. For some reason non-software engineers feel like there was some kind of gate-keeping preventing them for penetrating the domain. I am deeply
saddedned by this, as I am a self-taught software engineer. Most of the stuff that I learnt, and continue learning, is via free, community based resources. And how amazing that is!
Youtube is full of resources for learning CS, architecture, etc. You just need to put it the time to do it. 

